\section{ARR Responsible NLP Research Checklist}
\label{sec:checklist}

\subsection*{A. For Every Submission}

\begin{itemize}
\item[\textbf{A1.}] \textbf{Did you describe the limitations of your work?} Yes. \S Discussion, ``Limitations'' subsection covers dataset scope, model scale, seed variance, context overflow confound, and task generalization.
\item[\textbf{A2.}] \textbf{Did you discuss any potential risks of your work?} Yes. The work is low-risk: it classifies code execution outcomes using publicly available programming benchmarks with no human subjects, privacy-sensitive data, or dual-use concerns.
\item[\textbf{A3.}] \textbf{Do the abstract and introduction summarize the paper's main claims?} Yes. Both state the three core findings: exception signal transfer, information density decomposition, and the two-tier diagnostic hierarchy.
\item[\textbf{A4.}] \textbf{Have you used AI writing assistants?} Yes. We used Claude (Anthropic) for drafting assistance. All scientific claims, experimental design, and analysis were conducted by the authors.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{B. Did You Use or Create Scientific Artifacts?}

\begin{itemize}
\item[\textbf{B1.}] \textbf{Did you cite the creators of artifacts you used?} Yes. HumanEval \citep{chen2021evaluating}, MBPP-sanitized \citep{austin2021program}, Qwen-2.5-Coder \citep{hui2024qwen25coder}, DeepSeek-Coder \citep{guo2024deepseek}, LoRA \citep{hu2022lora}, and HuggingFace TRL are cited.
\item[\textbf{B2.}] \textbf{Did you discuss the license or terms for use of artifacts?} Yes. HumanEval is released under MIT License; MBPP-sanitized under CC-BY-4.0. Both model weights are open-access.
\item[\textbf{B3.}] \textbf{Did you discuss if your use was consistent with intended use?} Yes. Both benchmarks are designed for code generation and evaluation research, consistent with our usage.
\item[\textbf{B4.}] \textbf{Did you discuss whether the data contains PII or offensive content?} Yes. The dataset consists solely of algorithmic programming problems and their execution outputs; no PII or offensive content is present (Ethics Statement).
\item[\textbf{B5.}] \textbf{Did you provide documentation of the artifacts?} Yes. Dataset construction is described in \S\ref{sec:dataset}; training hyperparameters in Appendix~\ref{sec:training_details}. Code and data will be released publicly.
\item[\textbf{B6.}] \textbf{Did you discuss potential risks of the dataset?} NA. The dataset contains only code execution traces with no foreseeable misuse potential.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{C. Did You Run Computational Experiments?}

\begin{itemize}
\item[\textbf{C1.}] \textbf{Did you report the number of parameters?} Yes. Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B (7.6B parameters) and DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B (6.7B parameters) are used as base models. LoRA adapters add ${\sim}$42M trainable parameters ($r{=}16$, all linear layers).
\item[\textbf{C2.}] \textbf{Did you report error bars or confidence intervals?} Yes. We report Wilson 95\% confidence intervals for all accuracy results, multi-seed standard deviations ($n{=}3$ seeds: 42, 123, 456) for key conditions, and per-class precision/recall/F1 (Appendix~\ref{sec:per_class}).
\item[\textbf{C3.}] \textbf{Did you report the amount of compute?} Yes. All experiments use a single RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell GPU (94\,GB VRAM). Per-condition wall-clock times are reported in Appendix~\ref{sec:training_details}, Table~\ref{tab:train_time}.
\item[\textbf{C4.}] \textbf{Did you report implementation details sufficient for reproducibility?} Yes. Full hyperparameters are in Appendix~\ref{sec:training_details}, Table~\ref{tab:hyperparams}: LoRA $r{=}16$, $\alpha{=}32$, dropout$\,{=}\,$0.05, learning rate $2{\times}10^{-4}$, linear decay, warmup ratio 0.1, weight decay 0.01, 3 epochs, effective batch size 32, max sequence length 2048. Framework: HuggingFace TRL SFTTrainer. Seeds: 42 (primary), 123 and 456 (robustness).
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{D. Did You Use Human Annotators?}

\begin{itemize}
\item[\textbf{D1--D5.}] NA. No human annotation was involved. All labels are derived from automated program execution (pass/fail verdicts from test-case outcomes).
\end{itemize}
